Q: Is it possible to manually define a conversion for an enum class? A: <p>no... but, enum class is really just a short hand for class that contains an enum.
(this is kinda like the previous answers, but easier to use, me thinks🤔)</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>class InventoryError
{
public:
enum Error
{
    success = 0,
    slotAlreadyExists = -1,
    unreachable = -100,
} error;

// this is here just so you can construct it with something like:
// InventoryError lastError = InventoryError::success;
InventoryError(Error error):
error(error)
{}

// the epic conversion operator
operator int() const {
    return error;
}
};
</code></pre>
<p>tested on c++20 with clang on windows.</p>
<p>Solutions like this get easy to figure out, once you realize most stuff in c++ is just taking something you have to do all the time, and just making the compiler do it for you.
like how classes and methods, are just structs and functions with first argument being a pointer to it. And basically despite that shorthand being there, not using it is perfectly valid; not every nail needs a drill to be nailed in.</p>
